WEBVTT

00:00.091 --> 00:03.735
[SPEAKER_00]: [Automatic captions by Autotekst using OpenAI Whisper V3. May contain recognition errors.] It's not about overcoming your fear. It's about embracing it, figuring out

00:03.815 --> 00:11.765
[SPEAKER_00]: what's going on and then making yourself actually strong. And I see this in every sport I do. It's like the guys you show up and they're like, I'm

00:11.785 --> 00:17.591
[SPEAKER_00]: going to overcome my fear. I'm going to huck my carcass, whatever. They don't last very long. They either get killed or they wash out because

00:17.611 --> 00:26.226
[SPEAKER_00]: they are actually afraid. You're an idiot if you're not afraid. I believe risk-taking is individual and super important. Try, listen,

00:26.286 --> 00:33.748
[SPEAKER_00]: learn, adapt, do better. That's the kind of mantra that I, fighter pilots call it an OTA loop, observe, orient, decide, act. And, you know,

00:33.848 --> 00:40.631
[SPEAKER_00]: my personal one is like, you got to try. That's the first thing. Nothing cool happens if you don't take that little risk and try. And then you got

00:40.651 --> 00:46.533
[SPEAKER_00]: to get out there and you got to listen. Like, what's the world telling me? What's shaking? Like, what is really going on? Not the bullshit in my

00:46.573 --> 00:54.177
[SPEAKER_00]: brain. What's really going on? You know, you got to listen to it, figure it out. Then you got to learn. These things will happen. And have we built

00:54.197 --> 01:01.582
[SPEAKER_00]: the capacity to manage them or not? You know, you're going to get sucked into a QNIM if you are not paying attention to the environment around

01:01.622 --> 01:08.927
[SPEAKER_00]: you. This will happen. QNIMs are, you know, it's just part of flying. They're out there. But if you've developed the tools to pay attention to

01:08.967 --> 01:14.751
[SPEAKER_00]: your environment and look at your phone, now you're developing capacity to avoid this situation, not just go.

01:26.910 --> 01:34.053
[SPEAKER_01]: Bill Gatt, this is such a big honor for me to host you on Paragliding Atlas. Welcome to the show, sir. Thank you for having me. It's an honor to

01:34.093 --> 01:41.597
[SPEAKER_00]: be here. You're the next big thing, right? So it's going to be great. I'm looking forward to it. Before we got rolling here, we had, yeah, it's

01:41.617 --> 01:47.079
[SPEAKER_00]: going to, it'll be good. Something will happen. And before we get going here, just I enjoyed our conversation leading up to this as well. And

01:47.279 --> 01:53.142
[SPEAKER_00]: you're trying to do something cool and I admire that. So yeah, looking forward to it. Well, you happen to be one of the biggest inspiration

01:53.162 --> 02:01.406
[SPEAKER_01]: behind that dream. And you know, this also is the first topic that I wanted to dive into. Inspiration is something that we all seek when we

02:01.646 --> 02:09.589
[SPEAKER_01]: start visualizing our goals. I mean, of course, goal setting is one thing, finding meaning, but getting inspired is the one which sets the

02:09.630 --> 02:19.277
[SPEAKER_01]: ball rolling. So my dilemma, which I'm sure a lot of other pilots will also relate to is that when you start looking for inspiration, is there a

02:19.377 --> 02:27.724
[SPEAKER_01]: blueprint to finding the correct inspiration? Because of course, I can get inspired by some of the top pilots in the sport. And for example, if I

02:27.744 --> 02:34.010
[SPEAKER_01]: want to be an Olympian, it's like get inspired by an Olympian, start training like an Olympian, start acting, behaving like an Olympian,

02:34.050 --> 02:40.514
[SPEAKER_01]: and then you become an Olympian. Paragliding on the other hand. especially, you know, some of the few competitions. We have pilots who

02:40.554 --> 02:48.639
[SPEAKER_01]: have a huge time where they've put in their effort and resources, which we do not see. Yet the inspiration is just like it is in other sports as

02:48.659 --> 02:57.988
[SPEAKER_01]: well. While getting inspired, what is the way to do it safely? Oh, that's about 10 questions at once. So I know where to take that one. I mean, I

02:58.008 --> 03:07.601
[SPEAKER_00]: would say that the first thing to ask ourselves maybe is, is what do you actually want to do? Like, and for me, that question is, is what is the

03:07.641 --> 03:15.086
[SPEAKER_00]: coolest thing I can do with my life? What would be the ultimate thing? And it changes. You know, when I was, I left a pretty successful career in

03:15.166 --> 03:22.910
[SPEAKER_00]: publishing and research and other media PR stuff when I was in my late twenties to chase this dream of being an adventure sports athlete.

03:23.090 --> 03:29.993
[SPEAKER_00]: Cause that was the coolest thing I could imagine doing. And I was like, I'm willing to go back to waiting tables, to building houses. These are

03:30.013 --> 03:38.078
[SPEAKER_00]: all careers, things I've done to make money, you know, and I'm a hell of a dishwasher if it comes right down to it. But I am going to do these sports

03:38.238 --> 03:44.561
[SPEAKER_00]: to the best of my ability. And if I have to wash dishes or whatever to do it, then I'm going to do it. That is the coolest thing I can see doing with my

03:44.601 --> 03:53.866
[SPEAKER_00]: life. That's what I want to pursue. So you have to answer that question. And it's not always sports. Some people it is. But a lot of what I do is also

03:53.906 --> 04:02.211
[SPEAKER_00]: the business side of things and a lot of writing and a lot of filmmaking and a lot of things that are not doing the sports. The pyramid, the sports

04:02.251 --> 04:10.716
[SPEAKER_00]: are at the top, but the... 90% of it is not doing the sport. It's all the other stuff. So I think that inspiration is to answer that question for

04:10.736 --> 04:19.422
[SPEAKER_00]: yourself. And, you know, it's conflicting and you have to sit there in that conflict and in that lack of knowledge and try things out and go

04:19.502 --> 04:27.867
[SPEAKER_00]: sideways. You know, I've tried to be a pro athlete in my early 20s. I was a sponsored kayaker. And so I was kayaking, you know, getting free boats

04:27.887 --> 04:34.628
[SPEAKER_00]: to the occasional ticket or something to go kayaking. And I couldn't make a living at it. And I was also competing at a high level in sport

04:34.668 --> 04:41.370
[SPEAKER_00]: climbing comps. But all I could get for that was a free pair of shoes and the odd ticket. So I couldn't figure out a way to make it work. But by going

04:41.390 --> 04:50.152
[SPEAKER_00]: through this sort of publishing PR world, I then understood how to make it work as a pro athlete later. So it wasn't a linear path. And I don't

04:50.172 --> 04:57.656
[SPEAKER_00]: think it is for anybody else. But that basic question is, what's the coolest life I can imagine living that you really feel? It's not just

04:57.716 --> 05:04.039
[SPEAKER_00]: about like, you know, whatever it is for you. Everybody always has like, you know, eating ice cream, sitting in bed. It's like, it's like

05:04.079 --> 05:10.482
[SPEAKER_00]: whatever it is, but it's not actually, it's something you got to actually feel that like has meaning to you and then to go and try it. And

05:10.702 --> 05:17.165
[SPEAKER_00]: you have to figure that out. Nobody else knows that. You know, I quit my job to be a professional icicle climber, right? Let's remember this.

05:17.425 --> 05:23.530
[SPEAKER_00]: What a ridiculous job description. Because I was on TV for the X Games and I could turn that into media impressions and money. Everybody's like,

05:23.550 --> 05:30.896
[SPEAKER_00]: you're an idiot. That won't work. Everybody said that to me. And, you know, I've done it. And so it's not what everybody else says you can or

05:30.916 --> 05:39.961
[SPEAKER_00]: can't do. And how much are you willing to sacrifice, you know? This actually helps me clarify my question more, which is like when you are

05:40.061 --> 05:48.546
[SPEAKER_01]: chasing your, like you've set a goal and you're chasing that goal. Is there, there is a fine line between pushing too hard because we

05:48.586 --> 05:57.612
[SPEAKER_01]: naturally do want to excel at things that we like or we plan to. And then pushing too hard and going into self-destruction mode or destructive

05:57.652 --> 06:06.657
[SPEAKER_01]: pushing is very difficult to identify because hard work means failure. more results and in conventional sports like hockey soccer or running

06:06.757 --> 06:13.702
[SPEAKER_01]: it's like you know just just go out there and do the 10 000 hour theory or something but when it comes to chasing adventure sports pushing too

06:13.762 --> 06:24.308
[SPEAKER_01]: hard is a bit it has to be pulled back at some point so identifying that your inspiration isn't causing you to switch off and forget where the

06:24.388 --> 06:32.371
[SPEAKER_01]: fine line is drawn how do you answer how do you navigate that kind of a dilemma as an athlete who is growing up in these sports I think the first

06:32.411 --> 06:40.637
[SPEAKER_00]: thing is to have a realistic appreciation of both how dangerous the sport is. And this is an idea in statistics called the base rate. So this

06:40.697 --> 06:48.342
[SPEAKER_00]: is like just the base rate of accidents. And everybody says, well, I'm not the same as the base rate, but everybody kind of is. I'm like, well, I

06:48.362 --> 06:55.945
[SPEAKER_00]: do it differently. I got it off. I got the secret sauce, which is almost universally not very accurate. So how dangerous is this? And given

06:55.985 --> 07:04.728
[SPEAKER_00]: that, how do I want to proceed in this sport? And that is a you know, that's a it's an individual thing. What somebody is doing at a high level

07:05.108 --> 07:12.290
[SPEAKER_00]: competition or in a long XC flight where it really matters, you know, or a world record chase or whatever it is. You know, as long as you have that

07:12.450 --> 07:21.273
[SPEAKER_00]: knowledge that things don't go well pretty regularly in these high risk sports and you're making decisions with that knowledge, then things

07:21.413 --> 07:29.055
[SPEAKER_00]: generally get better. So another one that is really critical, another tool you can use is ask if you're making the decision off of probability

07:29.155 --> 07:37.137
[SPEAKER_00]: or off of consequence. And what I've seen in a lot of sports and a lot of industrial settings, I do a lot of industrial risk work and so on as well.

07:37.197 --> 07:44.360
[SPEAKER_00]: It's another part of my life. But the amateurs tend to make decisions based off of probability. So this will probably work out. And it

07:44.400 --> 07:50.582
[SPEAKER_00]: probably will. But if you're a professional, you're going to be exposed hundreds of thousands of times to that individual hazard. So if you're

07:50.642 --> 07:56.724
[SPEAKER_00]: contemplating, you know, should I chuck this turn really close to the hillside? You'll probably get away with it. But if you're thinking in

07:56.764 --> 08:04.488
[SPEAKER_00]: terms of consequence... and you know that you're going to do that a lot and start hitting those low probability numbers, then maybe you want to

08:04.528 --> 08:10.790
[SPEAKER_00]: give yourself an extra 10 meters in case you take a big deflation and you don't hit the hillside. So I tend to think in high risk, high hazard

08:10.810 --> 08:18.654
[SPEAKER_00]: environments in terms of consequence rather than probability. And can I increase my distance from that consequence? whatever it is. So if I'm

08:18.694 --> 08:28.822
[SPEAKER_00]: coming into a ridge, do I need to be on full bar or could I go back to feeling the glider and slowing it down? It might save me five seconds or 10

08:29.103 --> 08:37.629
[SPEAKER_00]: seconds if I stay on full bar, but I can decrease the consequence and severity, then I will. So thinking in terms of consequence rather than

08:37.689 --> 08:42.814
[SPEAKER_00]: probability. Probability is for people who are going to do something a few times. If you're going to be a professional, you need to think in

08:42.854 --> 08:50.737
[SPEAKER_00]: terms of that consequence and that number is going to hit. Everybody I know who's flown for more than 10 or 15 years, you know, has crashed. And

08:51.037 --> 09:00.139
[SPEAKER_00]: we all are going to do it. So right here, right now, your job is to avoid that consequence. And, you know, that's one way to think about it. And

09:00.159 --> 09:07.241
[SPEAKER_00]: then another tool that I think is really useful is to build capacity. And, you know, in risk management for a long time, it was here's the

09:07.281 --> 09:14.823
[SPEAKER_00]: problem and here's the mitigation and we're done. But most of our accidents don't come within this really defined box of, you you know,

09:14.903 --> 09:22.152
[SPEAKER_00]: hazard and mitigation. The serious ones tend to come when something unusual starts happening or something we haven't seen before. And so

09:22.332 --> 09:29.380
[SPEAKER_00]: how do you deal with that? And the answer is capacity. That's really, really helpful. So, you know, in paragliding, we do things like take a

09:29.621 --> 09:36.963
[SPEAKER_00]: SIV clinics. so that we have more capacity when our wings blow up. But you can develop capacity a lot of ways. And I think that's one of the main

09:37.023 --> 09:43.984
[SPEAKER_00]: reasons that I've survived as long as I have, is I've approached all the risk sports I've done, and I didn't have a term for it, I just did it this

09:44.064 --> 09:51.526
[SPEAKER_00]: way, as trying to build capacity. So in kayaking, you know, whitewater kayaking, my friend would flip me over while we're standing in a lake,

09:51.566 --> 09:59.088
[SPEAKER_00]: and then he'd try to hold the boat upside down, right? And if I did manage to roll up, he'd hit me in the head, you know, if you'd I was trying to knock

09:59.108 --> 10:09.232
[SPEAKER_00]: me back over. This sounds insane, but I don't miss rolls paddling to this day. And that was 40 years ago when I learned how to roll like that. I roll

10:09.332 --> 10:16.355
[SPEAKER_00]: up no matter what. And in paragliding, I did some really unusual things. Like I practiced crashing. People are like, you're an idiot. But I went

10:16.415 --> 10:24.022
[SPEAKER_00]: into a gymnastic facility, had mats on the floor, and I told the gymnast guy who happened to be a rock climbing friend of mine who was a gymnast

10:24.062 --> 10:31.008
[SPEAKER_00]: coach, I was like, how do you teach a gymnast to crash? Because gymnasts crash all the time, right? They do studs off bars and things. They get

10:31.048 --> 10:39.035
[SPEAKER_00]: lost in the air. How do they survive that? And he was like, okay, you got to translate this vertical momentum into horizontal momentum. And

10:39.135 --> 10:45.822
[SPEAKER_00]: here's how you do that. And so I'm in this gymnastics facility, jumping off this bar that's like three meters above the ground, wearing my

10:45.862 --> 10:53.609
[SPEAKER_00]: paragliding harness and learning how to crash. And when I crashed, you know, I got banged up, but I didn't break everything. And that's

10:53.809 --> 11:04.639
[SPEAKER_00]: probably because I had developed capacity to deal with that. So these are some tools. It's like, think first about consequences and not just

11:04.699 --> 11:13.647
[SPEAKER_00]: probability, and then develop capacity so that when you still get it wrong, you can have better outcomes. So those are some tools. Does that

11:13.667 --> 11:22.651
[SPEAKER_00]: answer your question? 100%. I think you're saying mitigating the risk in the most effective manner, which is making peace in your head, is the

11:22.691 --> 11:30.374
[SPEAKER_01]: way to navigate risk versus reward spectrum. So you're out there, your original question was you're out there and you're thinking, you know,

11:30.694 --> 11:39.097
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm in this competition and I'm going to do this glide into the low and, you know, if my glider blows up, you know, I could go in. But if I pull it

11:39.157 --> 11:46.480
[SPEAKER_00]: off, I'll win. And for me in that situation, the usual, you know, First of all, if I blow my glider up, I'm not going to have my glider. That's going

11:46.500 --> 11:53.365
[SPEAKER_00]: to be a pain in the ass. I'm going to have to call up Jim and say, really sorry, you know, can I get another glider or whatever? And then I might

11:53.385 --> 12:01.731
[SPEAKER_00]: get killed. And it's not about any one day. You know, it's if you're making that, if you make decisions like that. you're not going to live

12:01.771 --> 12:08.033
[SPEAKER_00]: very long. But if I look at the consequence and I'm like, okay, well, I could blow up and I could crash into those rocks and I don't have it. I'm

12:08.053 --> 12:15.395
[SPEAKER_00]: going to do three more circles in this thermal and then glide. And if I don't win, I'm okay with that. I made the decision that having a

12:15.435 --> 12:21.497
[SPEAKER_00]: functional glider and a functional spine was more important than winning that day. And when you back up just a little bit, of course,

12:21.617 --> 12:27.018
[SPEAKER_00]: that's really obvious, right? But if you're thinking about probability, it's like, I could probably get away with this. then you

12:27.038 --> 12:33.424
[SPEAKER_00]: don't make good decisions and you don't balance that risk and reward at all. And then, you know, if you've still decided to go for it, you take a

12:33.444 --> 12:40.650
[SPEAKER_00]: big frontal and you've practiced that 50 times in an SIV, then you can recover the glider fast or maybe you crash into the trees, but you don't

12:40.690 --> 12:48.557
[SPEAKER_00]: break yourself. So in that situation, knowing your goal is to survive that day. That's your first goal. Like if you land at the end of the day and

12:48.577 --> 12:54.602
[SPEAKER_00]: you survived, you can be frustrated, but at least you survived. But if you're weighing out those consequences and are thinking about

12:54.642 --> 13:01.766
[SPEAKER_00]: probability and don't have capacity, then you're going to get bad results. I think this does make it a bit more clear for me. But what I'm

13:01.826 --> 13:10.812
[SPEAKER_01]: really curious is I was racing in Macedonia with 150 other triple Cs and I was on a two liner C. I had just gone to two liner Cs, full bar, house

13:10.832 --> 13:18.638
[SPEAKER_01]: thermal. You know, we're racing towards the first turn point and I'm kind of shitting my pants because it's my first few days on a two liner C,

13:18.798 --> 13:25.462
[SPEAKER_01]: whereas everybody else around me is like 500 hour plus pilot at least. But there's a bit of a herd mentality coming around me that, hey, all the

13:25.502 --> 13:33.687
[SPEAKER_01]: gliders are sailing smoothly. There's no visible turbulence in the air. So the probability, I'm not talking about capacity here. This was a

13:33.727 --> 13:40.692
[SPEAKER_01]: bit stupid on my part. The probability of me going down is also almost negligible because everybody else is handling it well, regardless of

13:40.732 --> 13:48.937
[SPEAKER_01]: how much effort they have put in versus what I have put in. And guess what happens? I get a full frontal right away. Of you know thankfully i had the

13:48.977 --> 13:56.122
[SPEAKER_01]: capacity to deal with full frontals and then i pulled back i was not an official part of that competition so that's why it was easy for me to pull

13:56.162 --> 14:05.589
[SPEAKER_01]: back but what i do see is that regardless of how much ever practice i do something in me will say that hey man 150 other humans are going out and

14:05.629 --> 14:14.355
[SPEAKER_01]: pushing it and you are no less than any one of them so just go back on full bar as soon as possible so when your gut knows that you're doing

14:14.395 --> 14:23.563
[SPEAKER_01]: something out of your comfort zone yet the herd mentality is trying to you know push you towards it is there a way that you can be very honest with

14:23.603 --> 14:31.130
[SPEAKER_01]: yourself and say that you know what live to fly another day or is this something that only wisdom and age will drill into you as the time passes

14:31.527 --> 14:38.369
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, it's interesting. When I was younger, I always wanted to be more brave, to push harder and be more comfortable, you know, like the

14:38.409 --> 14:46.871
[SPEAKER_00]: other kids or whatever, just do something. And I'd be like, no, I can't do that, you know, and I would build up to it. So in your example right there,

14:46.911 --> 14:54.334
[SPEAKER_00]: I would say that if you're feeling that fear just because you're on full bar, you have not built the capacity to handle that situation well, and

14:54.354 --> 15:02.579
[SPEAKER_00]: you probably should be afraid. And if you're feeling that, I guarantee you that the person who's leading that day, the 10,000 hour pilot who is a

15:02.619 --> 15:09.424
[SPEAKER_00]: test pilot, he is not afraid. If you're feeling fear in that environment, a little bit of nervousness is fine. You know, that keys

15:09.484 --> 15:15.669
[SPEAKER_00]: you up, gets your central nervous system going, all good. A little bit's good. But if you're like honestly having fear, I have learned over

15:15.749 --> 15:23.575
[SPEAKER_00]: decades of high risk sports that if I am feeling fear, bluntly I'm fucking up and I need to step back, figure out why I'm feeling that fear or

15:23.655 --> 15:31.679
[SPEAKER_00]: detune the situation. You know, when I was 16 or 17 and paddling very serious first descents in Canada, I used to throw up in the eddies at the

15:31.719 --> 15:40.624
[SPEAKER_00]: top because I was so afraid to run the drop, right? And I paddled horribly as a result. You cannot paddle or fly a paraglider well when you're

15:40.704 --> 15:50.830
[SPEAKER_00]: afraid. So if you feel that fear, it doesn't matter what 150 people are doing. If you feel that fear... If you get off the bar and you're flying

15:50.890 --> 15:57.055
[SPEAKER_00]: along and you're like, well, this is really boring. I could be on a little bit of bar and you want to be on a little bit of bar. Great. Get on a bit of

15:57.075 --> 16:04.942
[SPEAKER_00]: bar. You're not even in the competition. It doesn't matter at all. What matters is what's going on on you. So get off the bar and When I started

16:04.962 --> 16:13.388
[SPEAKER_00]: paddling drops or flying in conditions that I wanted to fly in, then I performed way better. And that meant stepping back and not paddling

16:13.428 --> 16:21.934
[SPEAKER_00]: like class five first descents in the middle of nowhere, but paddling class three or four water and developing capacity in that water to hit

16:22.014 --> 16:31.160
[SPEAKER_00]: any line I wanted perfectly. And I am not afraid. I'm paying attention on a river today or in the air, but if I'm feeling afraid, man, that's a sign

16:31.200 --> 16:39.425
[SPEAKER_00]: that I need to back up, slow it down. It's not about overcoming your fear. It's about embracing it, figuring out what's going on, and then making

16:39.465 --> 16:46.389
[SPEAKER_00]: yourself actually strong. And I see this in every sport I do. It's like the guys you show up and they're like, I'm going to overcome my fear. I'm

16:46.409 --> 16:52.151
[SPEAKER_00]: going to huck my carcass, whatever. They don't last very long. They either get killed or they wash out because they are actually afraid.

16:52.271 --> 17:00.373
[SPEAKER_00]: You're an idiot if you're not afraid. Flying a paraglider in some conditions, you know, you should feel, I'm sorry, but if you don't feel

17:00.393 --> 17:06.854
[SPEAKER_00]: that, you're not going to last long. You're going to hit the hillside. You know, if you've got that fear, then ask why and back it up until you

17:06.914 --> 17:14.860
[SPEAKER_00]: don't have it and progress when you want it. You know, I remember I won the US Paraguay Nationals one year. We were all flying toward goal and I was

17:15.260 --> 17:22.106
[SPEAKER_00]: down by like six points or something. I knew I had to win the day by, especially against two other people on that meet. And we're flying

17:22.146 --> 17:32.794
[SPEAKER_00]: along and it's fairly rough and I'm on full bar and I blew my glider up. I did not care whatsoever. I was back on that bar within 15 seconds. Yeah,

17:32.814 --> 17:39.740
[SPEAKER_00]: that was, I wanted it and I was going for it and I was high. Whereas if I were racing in fear, I never would have won that comp. And I knew I could deal

17:39.780 --> 17:47.805
[SPEAKER_00]: with that glider blowing up. And it blew up. I was just annoyed that I was going to lose 50 meters. I'm like, I didn't care. I was like, got the

17:47.865 --> 17:54.730
[SPEAKER_00]: glider back on the park. That's where you want to be. The funny thing about that is that one of the guys I was racing against watched that

17:54.770 --> 18:04.016
[SPEAKER_00]: happen. And he watched me blow up. And then he, later I saw him at the award ceremony or whatever. And his exact, it was Bill Belcourt, another

18:04.056 --> 18:10.442
[SPEAKER_00]: well-known US pilot. And He said, if you want it that bad, man, I'm just going to give it to you because it was clear you were going to go. So

18:10.562 --> 18:18.769
[SPEAKER_00]: internally, I was at peace with it. And also as a competitive tactic, it was very clear that I was going to go that hard. But I was definitely not

18:18.809 --> 18:27.637
[SPEAKER_00]: afraid. You can't be existing in that space. That's a really long answer to a short question. But if you have fear about anything, embrace it. You

18:27.657 --> 18:34.041
[SPEAKER_00]: know, it's normal to be a little bit worried or to have a bit of a bump in your, you know, when I get up in front of a thousand people to talk, it's

18:34.121 --> 18:42.127
[SPEAKER_00]: something, man. I'm ready, but I've prepared well and I'm solid and I'm going to go do it. And if I'm feeling that on a paragliding launch or below

18:42.167 --> 18:49.351
[SPEAKER_00]: a big climb or at the top of a big drop, I'm not in. Like, I'm out. I don't want to feel that way. Quickly, before we move on to the next one, fear of

18:49.572 --> 18:57.157
