[SPEAKER_01]: best out of the situation kind of a thing? Like when you're in that kind of situation, so two things, you've got to do your best to be a participant

54:42.131 --> 54:50.058
[SPEAKER_00]: in that situation as opposed to like a package. So even when things have gone horribly wrong, a 30% improvement in outcome is often quite

54:50.098 --> 55:00.906
[SPEAKER_00]: meaningful, right? It's really, really important or 10% or 2%. It could be the difference. So to stay engaged and to stay focused on getting

55:00.946 --> 55:09.494
[SPEAKER_00]: things better. And in the industrial world, they have an acronym, which is STKY, S-T-K-Y. And the acronym stands for shit that can kill you,

55:09.754 --> 55:17.121
[SPEAKER_00]: sticky. And it's a really useful acronym. Usually when you're in one of those high intensity situations, there's only a few things that you

55:17.201 --> 55:22.606
[SPEAKER_00]: actually need to manage. You're not worried about your bank balance, your mortgage, your love life. You got to worry about like, am I going to

55:22.646 --> 55:29.891
[SPEAKER_00]: hit the hill? Like that'll kill you. And what am I going to do about it? How can I make this better? You know, if I flare really hard because I've

55:29.931 --> 55:36.835
[SPEAKER_00]: misjudged my circle, you know, I'll hit it less hard. Okay, that's what I'm going to do. You know, in your downwind landing situation, it's

55:36.875 --> 55:45.220
[SPEAKER_00]: like, if I fly into the high tension lines, that'll kill me. So I can't do that. Any outcome but that is probably best. So, you know, that... in

55:45.260 --> 55:54.205
[SPEAKER_00]: those high intensity situations, knowing what's going to kill you and avoiding that is probably the useful thing to do. But, you know, I just

55:54.225 --> 56:00.168
[SPEAKER_00]: wrote a big article about this, but the people always want to know what to do in these high intensity situations. Like, what do I do when I'm

56:00.188 --> 56:07.852
[SPEAKER_00]: getting sucked into a QNAM or I'm landing downwind in the, you know, my local LZ, what do I do? It's like, well, don't do that. You know, what

56:07.892 --> 56:16.737
[SPEAKER_00]: happened is you got sucked into a QNAM. You didn't get sucked into a QNAM, you flew into a QNAM. I have never seen anybody get sucked into a QNAP. You

56:16.757 --> 56:24.983
[SPEAKER_00]: know, they flew into them. I watched Ava go to 9,000 meters. She thermaled up underneath that QNAP. So rather than... It's useful to

56:25.023 --> 56:32.748
[SPEAKER_00]: think about what I'm going to do in these super extreme situations. But the best thing to do is to avoid them and to think about, you know, I've

56:32.768 --> 56:41.393
[SPEAKER_00]: thought a lot about this. It's like, okay, I flew the Texas dry line for 20 years. This is the biggest... QNIM tornado alley area possibly in the

56:41.433 --> 56:51.057
[SPEAKER_00]: world. Every day it's going to go ballistic. I'm planning to fly there. So what do I need to watch there so I don't fly into a QNIM? You know, this

56:51.077 --> 56:56.960
[SPEAKER_00]: really bothers me. People are like, well, I got sucked in. No, you didn't. You flew in. And once you start taking kind of real

56:57.020 --> 57:04.303
[SPEAKER_00]: responsibility for the outcomes, then you could fix those outcomes. But this is a problem, especially for some reason in paragliding where

57:04.343 --> 57:12.708
[SPEAKER_00]: people are like, you know, I got sucked into a cloud. The clouds suck. This is a bullshit. Like every cloud, it's a thermal that goes into it.

57:12.828 --> 57:21.353
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, don't do that. Like, yeah. Okay. How are you going to not do that? Well, I'm going to start my glide out earlier in a day when it's overcast

57:21.373 --> 57:28.277
[SPEAKER_00]: and I can't see the QNMs. I'm going to check the radar on my phone because you know what? You can do that in 90% of the world. Well, look at that. The

57:28.397 --> 57:38.842
[SPEAKER_00]: one 15K in front of me is now going to 12,000 meters. I probably should go land. So it's... Yeah. Anyhow, long rant about that, but do focus on

57:39.482 --> 57:47.305
[SPEAKER_00]: avoiding those situations and building knowledge to avoid them rather than just solving them. But people tend to want to solve them rather than

57:47.325 --> 57:56.107
[SPEAKER_00]: avoid them. Yeah. I think this is a bit personal to me because I think within my first year of flying, I was a 90-hour pilot or something. I had a

57:56.147 --> 58:05.973
[SPEAKER_01]: deployment over at Dharamsala in Bhutan. And that was just asymmetric, gone into cascade and went into double twisted auto rotation. So what I,

58:08.015 --> 58:17.101
[SPEAKER_01]: my brain said fight it until it reached G point where I was like, hey man, either deploy or you're passing out. You know, that maybe that blackout

58:17.121 --> 58:28.189
[SPEAKER_01]: was coming in. So my thing is, there was a point in time where I just accepted my fate and said that now it's either the handle or it's over. So

58:28.709 --> 58:39.116
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are going towards an impact or a final undesirable state, is it okay to give up and brace for impact? Or should you kind of keep on

58:39.656 --> 58:48.542
[SPEAKER_01]: fighting until the last bit? Because that has eaten me up since that day that, hey, why did you just give up when you were 2,000 meters at least to

58:48.602 --> 58:56.488
[SPEAKER_01]: clearance and bracing for impact? When should that come and should it be peaceful when that comes in your mind? So thank you for telling me that.

58:56.748 --> 59:04.331
[SPEAKER_00]: And it sounds like it was personal and emotional and still driving you a little bit today. So a couple of quick responses to that. The first is

59:04.351 --> 59:11.614
[SPEAKER_00]: that you did the right thing. You were in a situation that you did not see how it was going to get better and you're getting G'd out and you threw

59:11.634 --> 59:19.118
[SPEAKER_00]: your reserve. Most everybody I know that's thrown their reserve has walked away. A lot of people, I'd say most everybody I've known that

59:19.278 --> 59:27.162
[SPEAKER_00]: hasn't thrown the reserve, but has braced for impact, has gotten really messed up. So in my books, you did the right thing. You had a solution to a

59:27.222 --> 59:33.926
[SPEAKER_00]: problem. And if you're a very, very experienced pilot, maybe you have other solutions to that problem, but you didn't have the capacity

59:33.966 --> 59:40.590
[SPEAKER_00]: anymore. So you did the logical thing, which is throw your reserve. I think that's awesome. I wish more people did that. If more people threw

59:40.610 --> 59:47.515
[SPEAKER_00]: their reserves earlier, we'd have a lot less fatalities in paragliding. That is really clear to me. And people are like, oh, but you

59:47.535 --> 59:53.078
[SPEAKER_00]: could go into a power line. Well, sure you could, but you could go into the power line fighting your glider all the way into the dirt and hit the

59:53.118 --> 01:00:03.065
[SPEAKER_00]: tower going 120K an hour and break everything in your body. Very few situations are improved by riding the paraglider into the dirt. So you

01:00:03.105 --> 01:00:09.487
[SPEAKER_00]: threw early and I think that's awesome. I guess the second thing I'd ask you is a question. If you took that same asymmetric today, what would

01:00:09.507 --> 01:00:17.069
[SPEAKER_00]: happen to you? Easy. I hopefully know how to come out of it. And how do you know that? Just went out and put in the work. Yeah. So you've built

01:00:17.109 --> 01:00:24.111
[SPEAKER_00]: capacity to handle that. So your problem wasn't what, should I brace for impact or throw my reserve? Like these are not, neither one of these are

01:00:24.131 --> 01:00:31.212
[SPEAKER_00]: like, like by the time you've gotten to that point, you did the right thing. You threw your reserve. But the bigger picture here is again, to

01:00:31.272 --> 01:00:38.416
[SPEAKER_00]: build capacity. Asymmetrics are an expected part of paragliding. So are frontals on speedbar on two-line gliders. These things will

01:00:38.476 --> 01:00:46.521
[SPEAKER_00]: happen. And have we built the capacity to manage them or not? You know, you're going to get sucked into a QNIM if you are not paying attention to

01:00:46.561 --> 01:00:53.406
[SPEAKER_00]: the environment around you. This will happen. QNIMs are, you know, it's just part of flying. They're out there. But if you've developed the

01:00:53.466 --> 01:01:01.070
[SPEAKER_00]: tools to pay attention to your environment and look at your phone, now you're developing capacity to avoid this situation. Not just go. And I

01:01:01.130 --> 01:01:07.173
[SPEAKER_00]: read, I listened to this article with this guy who got sucked into a, he flew into a QNAP and he's like, I need a faster glider so I can fly away

01:01:07.433 --> 01:01:14.675
[SPEAKER_00]: faster. This is total bullshit. You need to not fly into QNAPs. You need to have the understanding of the systems. It doesn't matter whether

01:01:14.695 --> 01:01:22.278
[SPEAKER_00]: you're on a, you know, the hottest comp ship in existence or an A-wing. The difference is like not a lot, really, in terms of getting started.

01:01:22.638 --> 01:01:29.728
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the inflow to Acunium is going very, very fast. I've been in a couple of them. It's shit. I screwed up and you do not want to have this

01:01:29.788 --> 01:01:38.561
[SPEAKER_00]: experience. It doesn't matter what you're on. So, you know, avoidance is, capacity is good, but avoiding it is more, is more better.

01:01:41.379 --> 01:01:49.861
[SPEAKER_01]: Love it. Love it. Well, I think this is one of the most comprehensive talks we have had on staying safe and yet having fun without, you know,

01:01:50.002 --> 01:01:57.044
[SPEAKER_01]: normalizing or obsessing over risk assessment or something. And what you just said is really important. It's like people forget why

01:01:57.064 --> 01:02:03.288
[SPEAKER_00]: everybody wants to make paragliding safe and it's not safe. I don't care who you are or what you do, you're going to hit the ground sooner or later.

01:02:03.388 --> 01:02:09.214
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what happens. And some of the people are like, I've got it all figured out. I've got the perfect sauce. I fly safely. They hit the

01:02:09.254 --> 01:02:16.922
[SPEAKER_00]: ground, man. I've seen this happen several times, worked that over the years. So you got to think that this is fun and worth that possibility.

01:02:17.122 --> 01:02:24.888
[SPEAKER_00]: Like to me, it is. Flying paragliders is awesome. It's worth the risk to me. And I know that it's very likely that sooner or later I'll get it wrong

01:02:24.908 --> 01:02:33.189
[SPEAKER_00]: and I'll hit the ground. I'm not doing this safely. It's really dangerous. But I can make it somewhat safer and I can make good decisions

01:02:33.549 --> 01:02:40.231
[SPEAKER_00]: and focus on the process of making good decisions. And then if it goes bad, you know, I'll be like, well, I really did think about this and I made

01:02:40.271 --> 01:02:48.093
[SPEAKER_00]: a good effort and I, you know, I didn't hit it. But, you know, at least I put the work in and tried. So being process-intensive. driven, failing

01:02:48.153 --> 01:02:54.977
[SPEAKER_00]: safely. You know, some of the tools we've talked about, I hope they resonate a little bit with people and with you. And then the ultimate

01:02:54.997 --> 01:03:00.800
[SPEAKER_00]: goal is to come up with your own. I'm just making this shit up. It's like based on a lot of experience, but it's just my bottle and you got to

01:03:00.820 --> 01:03:07.706
[SPEAKER_00]: develop your own. Everybody does. And I wish them luck with that. Thank you for those words of wisdom and kindness for sharing them with us,

01:03:07.746 --> 01:03:16.413
[SPEAKER_01]: Will. And all I can say is that you, your lifestyle and the way you impart knowledge is an inspiration. So keep rocking and we will keep getting

01:03:16.453 --> 01:03:22.259
[SPEAKER_01]: inspired. Thank you once again. It's a pleasure. Thank you. Thanks. I'm really glad you threw your reserve, man. You're here. Good decision. So

01:03:22.399 --> 01:03:25.822
[SPEAKER_00]: right on. And I look forward to seeing you on a hill somewhere. Likewise, Will. Likewise.
